hadiths[1]{hadithnumber,text}:
"1263","Yaqoub Al-Dawraqi told us, Muhammad bin Musaab told us, Al-Awza’i told us, on the authority of Yahya bin Abi Kathir, on the authority of Muhammad bin Abdul Rahman bin Thawban, on the authority of Jabir bin Abdullah, said: The Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, used to pray while traveling wherever his mount was headed. So if he wanted to recite the obligatory prayer or the Witr prayer, he would make a vow and pray on the ground.” Abu Bakr said: Some people imagined that this narration indicated something contrary to the narration of Ibn Umar, and they used this narration as evidence that the Witr prayer was not permissible. On the move, and this is an error and an oversight on the part of whoever said it, and we do not have this news nor do we have anyone who distinguishes between the news that contradicts the news of Ibn Omar. The two reports are all agreed upon and used, and each of them reported what he saw the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, doing, and it is obligatory for those who knew Both reports are approved. Ibn Umar saw the Prophet, may God’s prayers and peace be upon him, tying the string on his camel, so he performed what he saw, and Jabir saw the Prophet, may God’s prayers and peace be upon him, tying his camel. So he performed Witr on the ground, and it performed what the Prophet, may God’s prayers and peace be upon him, saw. So it is permissible for a person to perform Witr on his camel, as he did, may God’s prayers and peace be upon him. It is permissible for him to mount his mount and disembark and perform the Witr prayer on the ground, since the Prophet, may God’s prayers and peace be upon him, did both deeds, and he did not refrain from doing so. One of them after doing it, and this is a difference in what is permissible. If the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, had not performed the Witr on the ground, but he had performed the Witr on the mounted one, it would have been impermissible for the traveling traveler to disembark and perform the Witr on the ground. But when the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, did both deeds, the one who was fastened with the choice while traveling, if he liked, was fastened on his mount, and if he wished, he disembarked. So he performed the Witr prayer on the ground, and nothing of his Sunnah, may God’s prayers and peace be upon him, is abandoned if it is possible to use it, but rather some of its experience is left alone. If it is not possible to use all of them, and one of them contradicts the other in all its directions, then it is necessary to request the transcriber of the two reports. And the abrogated from them, and the abrogated is used rather than the abrogated, and if it was permissible for someone to refute the report of Ibn Umar with the report of Jabir, it would be permissible for another to He supports the news of Jabir with the news of Ibn Omar; Because the narrations of Ibn Umar in the Witr narrations of the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, about the departed woman, there are more chains of narrations, more proven, and more correct than the narrations of Jabir, but it is not It is permissible for a scholar to contrast one of these two reports with the other. Rather, both of them are used as we have explained. The methods of Ibn Umar’s report have been mentioned in the book The big one"
